  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Multiple rounds of showers and thunderstorms \nare expected tonight through Wednesday, with heavy downpours and\nlocalized gusty winds.\n\nCurrent surface analysis depicts a low over southeastern \nWisconsin/northwestern Illinois early this morning, supporting a \nwarm front to extend southward from it and a cold front to extend \nwest and northwest across the Wisconsin/Illinois border. This low \nwill track across the central Great Lakes this morning before \nlifting east-northeast across New York state today. Ahead of the low \nan area of increasing mid-level winds, steep mid-level lapse rates \nand high PWAT values will continue to work its way across far WNY \nearly this morning. 0-6km wind shear will increase to between 20 and \n30 knots while remain less than 20 knots across the Genesee Valley, \nlimiting the severe thunderstorm potential to far western New York. \nThis being said, upstream convection, is beginning to work its way \neast across the region this morning, where numerous showers and \nthunderstorms will continue to move across the region through \nTuesday morning. Conditions are marginally favorable for an isolated \nsevere thunderstorm across far western New York early this morning, \nwith gusty winds will remain the favorable hazard. Additionally, due \nto the larger PWAT values, expect heavy rain with any stronger \nstorm.\n\nAs mentioned previously, the surface low will track across New York \nstate today, before becoming nearly stationary across north-central \nNew York Tuesday night through Wednesday. While showers will taper \noff across far western New York later this morning, diurnal heating \nwithin a very soggy environment will support additional showers and \nthunderstorms this afternoon. Meanwhile to the east, a low-level jet \nwill wrap around the surface low, supporting a few strong \nthunderstorms where heavy rainfall may lead to localized flooding \nacross areas of the Finger Lakes eastward into north-central New \nYork through this evening.\n\nAs the surface low settles across eastern New York tonight, wrap \naround moisture will support continued chances for showers across \nthe entirety of the forecast area. There is a Marginal Risk of \nExcessive Rainfall for portions of western and north-central NY \nthrough Wednesday.\n\nKey Message 2...Chances for showers and thunderstorms are expected \nto linger into Thursday, with drier conditions for the end of the \nwork week.\n\nAn anomalous strong area of surface low pressure will gradually \nslide east off the coast of southern New England Thursday. This will \nsupport the plume of moisture to pull east, however some diurnally \ndriven convection will be possible for areas inland from the lakes. \nOtherwise, expect cooler temperatures with highs in the 70s.\n\nHigh pressure will then move in across the eastern Great Lakes for \nfor the end of the work week supporting dry and seasonal weather.",
